  "query_preview": "period of quiet dry weather throughout the upcoming week \nwith temperatures rising back to above normal levels. The next \nthreat for rain will not come until next weekend as a cold front \nmoves into the region.\n\nThrough Tonight\n\nA renewed surge of broken stratus has expanded across much of the \nforecast area to the east of Interstate 65 early this morning. Skies \nwere clear to the west of I-65. 07Z temperatures ranged from the mid \n20s in parts of the Wabash Valley to the upper 30s in locations \nunder the stratus.\n\nThe deep upper trough continues to pull away to the east this \nmorning but cyclonic flow aloft persists on the back side across \nthe Great Lakes. This continues to aid in generating stratus \ndrifting S/SE across lower Michigan and the warmer waters of Lake \nMichigan. Even as drier air attempts to advect in from the west \ntoday...broken stratus is likely to linger across the northeast half \nof the forecast area through midday before slowly diminishing in \ncoverage and shifting east later this afternoon into the evening. \nCellular stratocu may develop on the western flank of the stratus \ndeck as well but the overall trend will be for decreasing cloud \ncoverage as the afternoon progresses with high pressure building in.\n\nThe expansion of the center of the high into the region tonight will \naid in further erosion of any lingering low clouds...but could also \nsupport some expansion of stratus back to the west into northeast \nportions of the forecast area in the predawn hours Monday as light \neasterly flow develops.\n\nTemps...highs today will range from the mid 40s northeast where \ngreater cloud cover is expected to the lower 50s in the lower Wabash \nValley. Low level thermals suggest slightly warmer temperatures \nSunday. Lows will range from the mid 20s to lower 30s both tonight \nand Sunday night.\n\nTemps...low level thermals support highs this afternoon generally 2-\n4 degrees warmer than Saturday in the lower and mid 50s. Lows \ntonight will again fall into a range from the mid 20s to lower 30s.\n\nMonday through Saturday\n\nThe strong surface high pressure will settle across the region for \nthe first part of the week...gradually shifting into the central \nAppalachians and parking itself through Friday and perhaps into next \nweekend. With broad ridging aloft establishing over the eastern part \nof the country as well...a tranquil weather pattern will persist all \nweek across the Ohio Valley with storm systems deflected overtop of \nthe ridge. Lingering boundary layer moisture may aid in diurnal cu \ndevelopment across the northeast half of the forecast area Monday \notherwise progressively deeper subsidence will develop with mostly \nclear skies. Any potential for rain will hold off until next weekend \nat the earliest at this point as a cold front approaches and \nattempts to buckle the eastern ridging. The onset of warm advection \nwill usher in a slow warming trend with highs rising into the 60s \nfor the second half of next week.",
